INT. HOSPITAL ROOM 412 - NIGHT

A single bed. An IV pole. A window onto a dark parking lot. The monitor's green line crawls.

DR. AMINA FAROUK, 40s, rumpled scrubs, white coat over her arm, sits on the edge of a chair, a tablet balanced on her knee. In the bed lies WALTER NKEMELU, 70s, a retired bus driver, oxygen cannula in his nose, one hand taped to a board.

Amina scrolls through the chart. Walter watches the ceiling tile.

AMINA
Your numbers are better than yesterday.

WALTER
(hoarse)
I'm a bus driver. I know what that means.

AMINA
It means better.

WALTER
It means you're being polite.

AMINA
I'm being accurate.

WALTER
That's the same thing in your line of work.

She sets the tablet down.

AMINA
(to the room, softly)
Okay.

WALTER
Who are you talking to?

AMINA
Nobody. Habit.

He turns his head on the pillow.

WALTER
You say that when you're about to tell me something I won't like.

AMINA
I do not.

WALTER
You do. Last Tuesday. Before you said the word "biopsy."

She picks at the cuff of her coat.

AMINA
The scan came back.

WALTER
And?

AMINA
And it's not what we hoped.

Walter says nothing. The monitor beeps. She speaks without looking at him.

AMINA (CONT'D)
(to herself)
Come on, Amina.

WALTER
Say it plain.

AMINA
It's spread. To the liver.

Walter closes his eyes. His fingers curl on the board, the tape pulling.

WALTER
How long?

AMINA
I don't want to guess.

WALTER
Guess.

AMINA
Months. Maybe six.

WALTER
Maybe.

AMINA
I'm sorry.

WALTER
Don't apologize. You didn't give it to me.

AMINA
No.

WALTER
Sit closer. I can't see you.

She slides the chair to the bed. He opens his eyes and finds her face.

WALTER (CONT'D)
My daughter's flying in Thursday.

AMINA
I'll make sure the visiting hours are flexible.

WALTER
I'd like to tell her myself.

AMINA
Of course.

WALTER
Not on the phone.

AMINA
Not on the phone.

He nods. Then, quietly, to the empty air:

WALTER (CONT'D)
(muttered)
Six months. Might as well finish the route.

Amina laughs, a short wet sound, and wipes her eye with her sleeve. Walter pats her hand with the one that isn't taped.

WALTER (CONT'D)
(to the room)
All right, then.

AMINA
All right.
